  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in S,$ there is a corresponding negative $2\\times 2$ matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 1=1,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
